; OUTLINE — udon-theory. Ordering lives here; identity lives in the slug.
;
; Conventions, gates, and the register discipline: FORMAT.md. Read it before writing a segment.
;
; EVERY ROW IS A GAP. No segment file exists. A row names a slot — its intended subject, its
; kind, and where its material already sits — and asserts nothing about the world. A row is
; not a summary of a claim; it is a description of a claim that would have to be established.
;
;   |segment[slug]  :type T  :max-attainable S  :stage gap  :see "P"  what this slot is about
;
;   :type    the kind of claim the slot is expected to hold (FORMAT §3). A proposal about the
;            slot, revisable by whoever writes it.
;   :status  ABSENT BY DESIGN. Strength is assigned by whoever reads the source at the primary
;            and writes the segment. A status assigned from an outline row would be a proxy
;            wearing verification's clothes (FORMAT §0).
;   :max-attainable
;            The ceiling — the strongest status this slot could EVER reach, however much work
;            goes in. Unlike :status this is warrantable here, because a ceiling follows from
;            the KIND of claim rather than from work done. The outline is authoritative for it;
;            a segment carries it as metadata and in its Epistemic Status prose. Two conventions:
;            a TRANSMITTED claim's ceiling is its source's own tier and never higher; a NORMATIVE
;            slot's ceiling is `decided`, since a decision is not truth-apt and saying so is more
;            informative than leaving it blank. A ceiling plus an absent status is a route: it
;            says what could be established here and that nobody has established it yet.
;   :stage   `gap` throughout. Becomes `draft` when a file exists.
;   :see     where the material already sits, as a bare Obsidian wikilink — [[stem]], never
;            [[stem#Heading]] — with a trailing plain-text §-marker where a location narrower
;            than the whole document is known: [[stem]] §9. No paths — stems are unique enough,
;            and a path is a location that rots where a stem is an identity. Splitting the
;            marker out of the link body means a heading can be renumbered or a target can be
;            a UDON element key, a table row id, or a bold sub-item — anything that isn't a
;            real heading anchor — without the wikilink itself going stale; the marker is honest
;            residue either way. That residue is real: 32 of 163 references here name a
;            sub-document target that no current reference form in this estate can address,
;            which is demand evidence for the paths work rather than a formatting lapse.
;            A source nobody has opened is integration debt. Where that debt threatens a
;            specific slot it is a |gap row beside the slot, naming the doubt rather than
;            the reader.
;
;   |gap     a named absence — the UDON spelling of the --GAP-- row convention, as an element
;            so it is addressable and queryable. A named absence is part of the structure; an
;            unnamed one is a hole nobody can see. Adding one is a contribution.
;   |arc     why rows sit in this order and what each supplies to the next. An arc makes no
;            claim about the world. An arc that asserts something is a defect, and the
;            assertion belongs in a row.
;
; The ordering is a proposal about organization, not a finding about it. Reordering costs
; nothing; renaming a slug costs everything downstream of it.

|outline[udon-theory] :version 0.1
  :governed-by FORMAT.md
  :segments-dir src/
  :purpose Claims that would resolve paths, schemas and rules, dialects, value typing, embed dispatch, and the tooling layer that consumes them
  :extraction Part IX is where normative consequences are lifted toward the spec. No row here is ruled

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[method]
    :title Method — the corpus's own arrangement
    |arc
      These slots state how this corpus works, so an auditor reading in outline order meets
      them before any substantive claim and can hold the rest to them. They are claims about
      the corpus, not about UDON, and they take labels for the same reason every row does.

    |chapter[arrangement] :title "Arrangement and mechanism"
      |segment[disc-collision-surface] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap :see "[[outline-segments-generalization-2026-07-23]] §Part 7"  Whether present-truth collision is the arrangement's active ingredient, and what its two relayed specimens support
      |segment[disc-named-absence] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap :see "[[outline-segments-generalization-2026-07-23]] §Part 7"  What collision structurally cannot detect, and why a named absence is the complementary instrument
      |segment[form-identity-ordering-split] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative :stage gap :see "[[asf/FORMAT]] · [[outline-segments-generalization-2026-07-23]] §Part 1"  Slug-carries-identity and outline-carries-ordering, and whether the separation requires the claims to form a partial order
      |segment[norm-different-repairs-test] :type normative :max-attainable decided   :stage gap :see "[[TST-extension-memo]] §2 · [[outline-segments-generalization-2026-07-23]] §Part 1"  The different-repairs test for whether two labels earn separation, and the three collapses it caught
      |segment[norm-proxy-discipline] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap :see "[[spikes.sop]] §0"  Provenance, indexes, consensus and convergence as proxies that locate a question without settling it
      |segment[norm-honest-discharge] :type normative :max-attainable decided :stage gap :see "[[spikes.sop]] §0c"  The three conditions under which an honestly lower-tiered landing is a complete discharge, and the verification-regress it prevents
      |segment[norm-absence-search] :type normative :max-attainable decided :stage gap   Why a universal negative is warranted only by its search, and what the narrow true form buys that the broad false one destroys
      |segment[norm-carriage-risk] :type normative :max-attainable decided :stage gap :see "[[method-evidence-tiers]] §lock-2"  The two independent grounds on which a transmitted claim is defeasible
      |segment[norm-transfer-obligation] :type normative :max-attainable decided :stage gap :see "#asf/tst/obs-software-epistemic-properties"  The obligation on exporting a theory result, and which properties the document domain shares, approximates and lacks
      |gap :candidate form-label-axes-for-a-derivation-corpus :see "[[method-evidence-tiers]] · [[type-algebra]] §0"  Which axes this corpus needs. ASF's trio travels to 02-TST untranslated; the demand corpus's support-kinds are calibrated for surveyed evidence, where the working distinction here has been derived-here versus transmitted versus spec-grounded
      |gap :candidate der-ratified-axes-as-segments :see "[[for-OUTLINE]] §11 · §76 · §78 · §82"  The three axes and two locks are ratified content held in a chapter its own corpus records as overloaded and pre-segmentation; segmenting them is the split that corpus has not run, and OPEN's SEG-SPLIT is the question it would answer
      |gap :candidate norm-corpus-register-discipline  Whether the register vocabulary needs a kind for a claim about the corpus itself as distinct from a claim about UDON

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[why] :n I
    :title Why a foundation exists, and what would count as success
    |arc
      The scope layer: what kind of thing this stack is, and how success would be recognized,
      before any machinery arrives. Most slots here are transmitted, so each depends on
      #norm-transfer-obligation for its borrowing.

    |chapter[epistemic-manufacture] :title "The epistemic-manufacture frame"
      |segment[intro-udon-theory] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap   Opening: what this corpus joins and where it lands. Forward references to later parts are intended
      |segment[scope-document-epistemics] :type scope :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap :see "#asf/tst/obs-software-epistemic-properties · [[tst-grounding]] §2"  Which of the six epistemic properties documents hold, approximate and lack, and at what tier the source states them
      |segment[der-checker-intervention-channel] :type derived :max-attainable discussion-grade      :stage gap :see "[[tst-grounding]] §2b"  Whether a conformance checker supplies the intervention channel documents lack, and under what premises
      |segment[der-format-causal-structure] :type derived :max-attainable discussion-grade      :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §2"  Whether identity, typed edges and declared vocabulary constitute declared causal structure
      |segment[der-git-outcome-function] :type derived :max-attainable discussion-grade :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §2"  Whether checker-plus-version-control is a deterministic re-runnable outcome function, and how far its coverage reaches
      |segment[result-epistemic-manufacture] :type result :max-attainable discussion-grade       :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §2"  The stack stated as the manufacture of absent epistemic properties for the document domain, and what the statement subsumes
      |segment[disc-judgment-ceiling] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §2 · [[schema-ideation]] §6.1"  What no manufactured channel reaches, and the measured ceiling that bounds every claim below it
      |arc
        The three manufacture slots each name one absent property and one supplier; the project
        statement is their conjunction and depends on all three. The ceiling slot sits last
        because it bounds them rather than qualifying any one.

    |chapter[success-criterion] :title "What would count as success"
      |segment[der-ambiguity-lever] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "#asf/llm/scope-observation-ambiguity-modulation · [[theory-of-agentic-tooling]] §2.1"  The bias bound, which of its two factors a designer can move, and the named premises
      |segment[der-imported-persistence] :type derived :max-attainable exact      :stage gap :see "#asf/aat/der-turnover-information-recursion · [[persistence-is-imported]]"  The reinjection-channel result and what it forecloses about in-session substitutes
      |segment[der-interface-latency] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[theory-of-agentic-tooling]] §2.3"  Where tool-loop tempo sits in the persistence condition, and what follows for interface quality
      |segment[der-turnover-multiplier] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[tst-grounding]] §3.2"  Whether the turnover multiplier makes the payment options asymmetric rather than a plain trade
      |segment[norm-tempo-criterion] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap   What measurement would count as success for this stack, and what would falsify the framing
      |gap :candidate meas-checker-tempo-delta :see "[[tst-grounding]] §2b · [[empirica]] · [[test/usability]]"  Searched: ASF's empirica registry holds a run validating a tempo claim and a contract for citing it; the 2025-12 usability harness measured agent enablement over UDON contexts; the demand corpus carries one measured comprehension result with a partial non-replication. None measures a checker's effect on tempo over a live UDON corpus
      |gap :candidate obs-transfer-table-checked-segment-by-segment  The properties table for documents was assembled in one pass; its rows have not been checked against the source segments individually

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[population] :n II
    :title The population and its serving
    |arc
      What a UDON corpus is logically, before schema or address. Order runs population, then
      layout criterion, then the roles that map one onto the other, because each group uses
      the vocabulary the previous one defines.

    |chapter[population-and-serving] :title "Population and serving"
      |segment[def-population] :type definition :max-attainable axiomatic   :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §1"  A collection as a declared population: typed elements, identity space, labeled edges, and where order is content
      |segment[def-serving] :type definition :max-attainable axiomatic   :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §1"  A serving as an assignment of the population to files, directories and regions
      |segment[form-placement-encoding] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative  :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §1"  Whether placement encodes a declared property rather than being it, and what a rot-resistant encoding buys
      |segment[scope-changeset-atom] :type scope :max-attainable axiomatic        :stage gap :see "#asf/tst/def-atomic-changeset"  The changeset as the atom, and what a file becomes when measured against it
      |segment[der-serving-criterion] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[acid-for-corpora]] §2"  Atomicity as feasibility constraint and coherence as objective, and the two decision procedures the field already built for it
      |segment[der-model-channel-split] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §1"  Whether the split is a model-channel split, and what serving-shaped tools cost each reader
      |arc
        #der-serving-criterion depends on the atom slot above it and on the refuted
        identification catalogued at #app-atomicity-coherence, which carries
        why the earlier form fails.

    |chapter[file-roles] :title "File roles"
      |segment[def-role-atomic] :type definition :max-attainable axiomatic   :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  Role (a): file as one record, filename as key
      |segment[def-role-multi-record] :type definition :max-attainable axiomatic   :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  Role (b): many records as true siblings in one file, and what the declaration adds that recognition cannot
      |segment[def-role-snippet] :type definition :max-attainable axiomatic   :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  Role (c): the snippet as the interior of an element whose opening line lives in the host
      |segment[def-role-view] :type definition :max-attainable axiomatic   :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  Role (d): a stored query that carries its own data, and the generated-versus-authored split
      |segment[der-role-set-semantics] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §I"  Whether the roles are three set-theoretic relationships rather than four file formats
      |segment[der-snippet-seam-closure] :type derived :max-attainable conditional    :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  What closes at the frontmatter seam under the snippet reading, and what the shared address space buys
      |segment[der-markdown-snippet] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  Whether every markdown file is already a role-(c) snippet, and what interring means if so
      |segment[form-interring-rules] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative  :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  The escape pass and the structure-free-file warning, and whether both are reversible and idempotent
      |segment[der-view-edge-attributes] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  Whether a view's per-record fields belong to the membership edge, and the correction story the field already ran
      |segment[obs-multi-record-lost-update] :type observation :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[acid-for-corpora]] §role-b"  What a concurrent append under role (b) produces, why it does not appear in a diff, and the prior art's verdict on the shape
      |segment[disc-view-relative-address] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap :see "[[underlying-logical-model]] §5"  Addressing by identity versus addressing through a view, and whether the fragility belongs to the view
      |gap :candidate der-role-declaration-surface :see "[[CARVEOUTS]] §PRAGMA"  How a role is declared — filename designator, pragma, collection default — undecided and landing on PRAGMA
      |gap :candidate der-snippet-root-attributes :see "[[DECISIONS]] L1"  Whether a snippet's root attributes carry meaning; gated on the root-attribute ruling, whose own rationale a declared role may answer

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[algebra] :n III
    :title The algebra of the model
    |arc
      The model restated as a two-sorted regular system. Parts IV and V consume what is
      established here, so the no-go chapter sits in this part rather than in an appendix — it
      fences what those parts may assume.

    |chapter[two-sorts] :title "Two sorts"
      |segment[intro-the-algebra] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap   Opening: what having an algebra rather than a schema language supplies, and what the two sorts are
      |segment[der-two-sorts] :type derived :max-attainable exact      :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §0.1"  Row types over per-key stack languages, hedge languages over content, and why faithfulness requires two sorts
      |segment[result-sort-boundary] :type result :max-attainable robust-qualitative    :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §0.1 · [[CORE]] §6.1  (0.9.1)"  Whether the sort boundary coincides with the attribute-versus-child test, and what follows if it does
      |segment[result-trace-quotient] :type result :max-attainable exact    :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §2"  The stacking hash as a trace-monoid quotient isomorphic to a product of per-key free monoids, and exactly what the quotient forgets
      |segment[form-commuted-by-default] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative  :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §2"  Cross-key order as conformance-invisible by default, and why the election is whole-row
      |segment[der-four-value-states] :type derived :max-attainable exact      :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §2 · [[CORE]] §11.4  (0.9.1)"  Absent, nil, stacked and list as distinct denotations, and why an algebra over the ergonomic view is unsound
      |segment[der-designated-keys-as-fields] :type derived :max-attainable exact :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §2"  Identity, traits and flags as ordinary row fields, and what needs no separate machinery as a result

    |chapter[what-the-algebra-decides] :title "What the algebra decides"
      |segment[result-shorthand-decidability] :type result :max-attainable exact :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §5 R2"  Decidability across the shorthand fragment, where componentwise checking fails, and the encoding that recovers it
      |segment[der-difference-types] :type derived :max-attainable exact :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §4"  Closure under union, intersection and complement, and the existence of difference types
      |segment[der-verdict-composition] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §5 R3"  What total recognition gives the judge, and the shape of a verdict that survives anomalies
      |segment[der-commutation-fence] :type derived :max-attainable exact  :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §5 R2"  Why the normalizing encoding is internal to the checker and would violate the serializer contract if emitted

    |chapter[discriminators] :title "Discriminators, anonymity, elision"
      |segment[result-discriminator-ladder] :type result :max-attainable exact       :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §9"  Name, key, traits, row shape and content shape as one family ordered by check cost
      |segment[der-disjointness-obligation] :type derived :max-attainable exact   :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §9"  Decidability of sibling disjointness per content position, and disjoint-or-discriminated as enforceable
      |segment[der-discrimination-window] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §9"  The content-phase boundary as the deadline for single-pass dispatch, and reporting how early a pair resolves
      |segment[result-elision-privilege] :type result :max-attainable exact :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §9"  Under what condition anonymity is safe, and what the disjointness check requires when it stops being
      |segment[disc-dispatch-resolution-parity] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §9"  Type-level dispatch and key-only reference resolution as one condition at two layers

    |chapter[boundaries] :title "Boundaries and open elections"
      |segment[der-typed-holes] :type derived :max-attainable exact      :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §6 N5"  What an interpolation in value or identity position does to membership, and the stratified verdict it forces
      |segment[obs-unit-element] :type observation :max-attainable empirical  :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §9"  The absence of a spelling for the pure group element, and the consumer population the blocking incumbent protects
      |segment[disc-flag-key-election] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §6 N6 · [[DISCUSSION-THOUGHTS]] O17"  The optionality-marker collision, its consumer count, and the three directions the election is open in
      |arc
        What remains here are not no-gos but the algebra's honest edges: a boundary the dynamics
        tier forces, an absence in the spelling space, and an election left open at zero
        consumers. The no-gos themselves are appendix evidence — #app-inference-floor and its
        siblings — because body claims depend on them rather than the reverse.

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[costumes] :n IV
    :title One algebra, four costumes
    |arc
      Four directions through the object Part III establishes. The co-occurrence chapter
      depends on the whole first chapter plus the failure-archaeology slot it cites; the gap
      row closing it names the unread evidence its two newest slots rest on.

    |chapter[the-unification] :title "The unification"
      |segment[result-intensional-subsets] :type result :max-attainable exact     :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §I · [[type-algebra]] §8"  Whether schemas, views, queries and censuses are intensional subsets of one population, closed under the boolean operations
      |segment[der-schema-composition] :type derived :max-attainable exact   :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §8"  A schema as a type plus the seats data cannot fill
      |segment[der-census-cardinality] :type derived :max-attainable exact :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §8"  A census as difference-type cardinality, and burn-down as model-counting
      |segment[der-coexistence-union] :type derived :max-attainable exact     :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §8"  A migration window as a union type rather than a convention
      |segment[der-path-selection-type] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §3"  A path's selection half as a type with declared arity bounds, and what that implies about two addressing languages
      |segment[der-mutation-decomposition] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §I"  Whether update exists at the semantic layer, and what append-plus-supersede inherits from stacking
      |segment[disc-shared-metaphysics] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §I"  Keep-everything and severity-by-loss read as event sourcing's axiom reached from the data-preservation side

    |chapter[the-co-occurrence-boundary] :title "The co-occurrence boundary"
      |segment[der-grammar-co-occurrence-limit] :type derived :max-attainable exact :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M13"  What a grammar-based schema language structurally cannot state, and where the ecosystems that shipped grammar-only put those constraints instead
      |segment[result-shorthand-boundary] :type result :max-attainable exact :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §5"  Whether boolean closure denotes finite co-occurrence over declared keys, where the remaining gap sits if it does, and whether the external pass's fetched primaries support the narrowing this slot claims
      |segment[der-seat-co-occurrence] :type derived :max-attainable exact :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §8"  Whether the declared-closed-carries-its-reason seat is itself a co-occurrence constraint, and whether the shorthand can express it
      |segment[hyp-typed-extraction] :type hypothesis :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M13"  Whether the disjointness obligation supplies the stable element-to-type map full regular tree grammars could not, and at what cost
      |segment[der-purity-insufficiency] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M13"  What separating validation from typing does and does not supply, and where the strategic risk actually sits
      |segment[form-arity-axis] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative  :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §3 · [[type-algebra]] §5 R4"  The four arity bounds at four sites, and how bounds compose through nesting
      |gap :candidate der-assertion-tier-shape  The tier arithmetic and cross-document constraints require is unshaped
      |gap :candidate der-shorthand-co-occurrence-spelling  No spelling exists for the union-of-rows case, which is what makes the fiat seats currently unwritable

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[resolution] :n V
    :title Resolution
    |arc
      One mechanism, then the addressing circuit that consumes it, then identity-over-time,
      which both depend on and which currently has no owner.

    |chapter[one-mechanism] :title "One mechanism, four customers"
      |segment[result-registry-resolution] :type result :max-attainable robust-qualitative      :stage gap :see "[[paths-ideation]] §3b · [[dialects-ideation]] §4"  Whether dialect labels, verbatim labels, references and filename designators are one mechanism resolving a name against a declared registry, and whether the shipped designator machinery has the shape this depends on
      |segment[der-kinded-failure] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[doc-store-and-schemas-report]] §8"  What a result carrying its kind on failure makes possible that a bare failure does not
      |segment[form-designator-rigidity] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative  :stage gap :see "[[doc-store-and-schemas-report]] §8"  The designator rigidity spectrum, and resolution-as-migration as a recorded lifecycle
      |segment[der-failure-vocabulary] :type derived :max-attainable conditional  :stage gap :see "[[paths-ideation]] §2d"  Four failure names, the four repairs they route to, and what one name covering two costs
      |segment[der-name-minting] :type derived :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[dialects-ideation]] §4"  Whether the name-minting question has an answer in machinery already running, and what the registration-tree prior art adds

    |chapter[addressing] :title "Addressing as one circuit"
      |segment[obs-speaking-occasions] :type observation :max-attainable empirical  :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §3"  The seven places a path is uttered, their speakers and media, and what each demands of a spelling
      |segment[der-pasteback-closure] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §3"  The condition under which read and edit are one loop, and what a tool's output must satisfy
      |segment[der-reference-subset] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §3 · [[DECISIONS]] S14"  Whether in-document references are a true subset of one address language, and what a second dialect of addressing would cost
      |segment[der-resolver-only-v0] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §3"  Which of the seven vectors touch the recognizer, and what a resolver-plus-convention could cover without a new recognition rule
      |segment[form-expectation-bounds] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative  :stage gap :see "[[DISCUSSION-THOUGHTS]] O15"  Operational and epistemic bounds as two jobs, and divergence as a dialogue trigger rather than a failure
      |segment[der-preregistered-expectation] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §3 · #asf/aat/der-loop-interventional-access"  What a bound stated before the act removes at the comparison moment, and what it does not restore
      |segment[der-cascade-enforcement] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §3 · #asf/aat/der-orient-cascade"  Whether a tool contract can enforce belief-update before re-planning where a norm cannot
      |gap :candidate der-bare-form-chaining-joiner :see "[[DISCUSSION-THOUGHTS]] O13"  Space terminates bare tokens, so the fused bare spelling needs a joiner or forgoes chaining

    |chapter[lineage] :title "Identity over time"
      |segment[obs-lineage-ownerless] :type observation :max-attainable empirical  :stage gap :see "[[schema-ideation]] §4 · [[CORE]] §1.1  (0.9.1)"  What identity-over-time claims, and which row of the owner table holds it
      |segment[der-lineage-presupposition] :type derived :max-attainable exact   :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §8"  What the difference type presupposes about a renamed key, and which slots become undefined without it
      |segment[der-lineage-keying] :type derived :max-attainable conditional    :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §V"  What identity through rekey, split and merge does to a document event stream
      |gap :candidate der-lineage-owner :see "[[schema-ideation]] §4"  Two placements are laid out with their consequences and neither is chosen; three slots in this outline depend on the choice

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[fiat] :n VI
    :title Fiat, and where data stops
    |arc
      Four principles about what observation supplies and what it cannot, then the design
      criterion they jointly force. Each depends on #app-inference-floor for its first clause
      and owes its own honest maximum for observation.

    |chapter[the-strata] :title "The four strata"
      |segment[post-descriptive-normative-split] :type postulate :max-attainable axiomatic :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §4"  The descriptive-normative distinction the rest of the part accepts, and what it declines to argue for
      |segment[der-p1-closure] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §4 · [[type-algebra]] §6 N1"  What observation can supply about vocabulary exhaustion, and what remains an act. An earlier form asserted closure was unobservable and was falsified by missing-mass estimation
      |segment[der-p2-law] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §4"  Enforcement records as evidence, adoption as the act, and how much of the record is not retained
      |segment[der-p3-reasons] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §4"  Where reasons exist in data at all, and which kinds existing schema languages have slots for
      |segment[der-p4-bindingness] :type derived :max-attainable empirical      :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §4 · [[extraction-probe]] §2"  What extraction does to incidental convention at scale, and what an adjudication state is for
      |segment[der-decision-layer] :type derived :max-attainable conditional  :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §4"  What the authored schema principally contains once the description layer regenerates on demand
      |segment[der-arguable-constraint] :type derived :max-attainable conditional  :stage gap :see "[[DISCUSSION-THOUGHTS]] O10"  What a reason changes about a constraint's relation to its reader
      |segment[obs-rationale-quadrant] :type observation :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[external-rationale-structure-2026-07-29]]"  What two literature lineages built toward reasons-as-structure, how far each went, and what the search that found the gap covered
      |segment[norm-undecided-default] :type normative :max-attainable decided :stage gap :see "[[late-misc-synopsis]] §4"  The default state for an extracted regularity, and why a declared bound is not an observed extremum
      |gap :candidate der-capture-problem-is-adoption-law  Reasons-as-structure carries an adoption constraint the literature names; no slot here clears it

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[governance] :n VII
    :title Membranes, clocks, and grain
    |arc
      The apparatus borrowed here is exact for continuous dynamics and document strata are
      discrete, so #norm-transfer-obligation binds every slot in the first
      chapter, and the gap row closing it names the premise that fails.

    |chapter[membranes-and-clocks] :title "Membranes and clocks"
      |segment[def-strata-tower] :type definition :max-attainable axiomatic   :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §3"  Five layers and the membrane each adjacent pair already carries
      |segment[form-membrane-dials] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §3"  One template with a clock ratio and a sensitivity, and the governance menu read as enforcement substrates for dial settings
      |segment[der-condition-repair-split] :type derived :max-attainable robust-qualitative      :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §3 · #asf/aat/der-multi-timescale-stability"  Two condition violations, why their repairs do not substitute, and what the tempting merge conceals
      |segment[der-permeable-membrane] :type derived :max-attainable conditional   :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §3"  Recognition read as a membrane at full permeability, and why refusal need not live in the parser
      |segment[der-vertical-scope] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §3"  Which questions the template answers, and which draw from composition machinery or partition instead
      |segment[hyp-semifreeze-dial] :type hypothesis :max-attainable empirical   :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §3 · [[DECISIONS]] C8"  What a semi-freeze does to canon sensitivity during high churn, and what the warm-start refinement prices
      |gap :candidate obs-shared-capacity-couples-the-clocks :see "[[db-theory]] §3"  The layers draw on one attention pool, violating the theorem's per-level capacity premise

    |chapter[time-and-grain] :title "Time, grain, channels"
      |segment[form-commit-fold] :type formulation :max-attainable robust-qualitative  :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §II"  Commits read as a transaction log, and which temporal verbs become folds over commit ranges
      |segment[der-verb-grain] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3.10"  File grain versus record grain per verb, and what the prior art says about which instrument fits which
      |segment[der-event-composition] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §II"  What a schema-aware diff supplies, what no diff supplies, and where the remainder must be captured
      |segment[der-channel-count] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M8 · #asf/aat/deriv-tempo-additivity"  When gates are one channel wearing several badges, and the signed correction the source makes to the general claim
      |segment[obs-declaration-free-channel] :type observation :max-attainable robust-qualitative :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §3 · [[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M8"  What extraction-based drift detection can catch that declaration-reading channels cannot
      |segment[der-substitution-table] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3.11"  Disposition and scope as two independent knobs, and what stating them converts
      |gap :candidate der-record-grain-escape-hatch :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3.10"  #app-second-store and record-grain history are in tension that current scale conceals
      |gap :candidate der-erasure-meets-append-supersede :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3.13"  Supersede is not erasure while prior versions remain reconstructable, and the common workaround's sufficiency is contested

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[minefield] :n VIII
    :title The minefield as constraint
    |arc
      Prior failures stated as mechanisms rather than histories, because a mechanism
      constrains design. The source marks its own evidence per claim; three of its mines rest
      on theory plus recall rather than fetched primaries, and a segment inherits that mark.

    |chapter[the-mines] :title "Thirteen mechanisms"
      |segment[intro-the-minefield] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §1"  Opening: the five recurring structural moves, and which four UDON is defended against by construction
      |segment[der-frozen-set-kind] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M1"  Type sniffing as a non-injectivity floor, and why a frozen set is a different object rather than a disciplined table
      |segment[der-silent-corner] :type derived :max-attainable conditional    :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M2"  Loud versus silent as the repair-relevant axis, and what a silent misparse does to update gain
      |segment[result-specified-recovery] :type result :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M2"  Which axis the historical move actually travelled, and which half of keep-everything that makes load-bearing
      |segment[der-repair-regimes] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M3"  Four repair-distinct regimes, their orthogonality to severity-by-loss, and the shipped design pattern for the hardest one
      |segment[der-convention-certificate] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M4"  A behavioral bound against a certificate, and why the step function explains a decade-late discovery
      |segment[der-additivity-requirement] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M5"  What an unbounded settling time does to the admissible spec-change rate, under the stated modeling identification
      |segment[der-blame-radius] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M6"  Declared bounded extents as observable intermediates, and the intractability they sidestep rather than solve
      |segment[der-spec-length] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M7"  What a specification's length measures, and what the shared-context term does to an adoption floor
      |segment[der-inert-reference-freeze] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M9"  An unresolved reference as an unobservable edge, and what one trivial resolver is an instrument for
      |segment[obs-collapse-catalog] :type observation :max-attainable empirical  :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M10"  Five merges of repair-distinct things, and the repair each merge conceals
      |segment[der-conformance-target] :type derived :max-attainable conditional  :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M11"  Why a grammar defined by an implementation has no fixed point, and which failure mode that names
      |segment[der-positional-commitment] :type derived :max-attainable conditional :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M12"  Escaping as behavioral against position as structural, and what the difference is in kind
      |segment[obs-host-profile-fork] :type observation :max-attainable empirical  :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §6.10"  How two conformant hosts can disagree with no spec move, and why this is a distinct failure class

    |chapter[standing-on-mines] :title "Where UDON stands on one"
      |segment[obs-defense-kinds] :type observation :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3"  Which commitments are certificates, which are conventions, and how each should be budgeted
      |segment[obs-anomaly-cascade] :type observation :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3.3"  What one unclosed construct produces without a root-versus-derived distinction, and which regime that manufactures
      |segment[obs-evaluation-channel] :type observation :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3.6"  The shape the dynamics tier shares with template injection, and at which layer the historical instances became incidents
      |gap :candidate obs-measured-indentation-failure  Two independent passes marked measured indentation-failure rates as unreached

  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |part[consequences] :n IX
    :title What the foundation forces
    |arc
      Each slot here is a normative consequence of a named slot above, separated so it can be
      lifted toward the spec without carrying this corpus's register with it. No slot here is
      ruled; the ledger and the steward carry that.

    |chapter[spec-delta] :title "Consequences for the spec"
      |segment[norm-lineage-owner] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap   Following #obs-lineage-ownerless: an owner row, or a carve-out entry with its closing condition
      |segment[norm-schema-carveout] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap   Following Part IV: the schema layer is the only major deferred layer with no carve-out entry, and three mines converge there
      |segment[norm-resolution-statement] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap   Following #result-registry-resolution: label-to-handler resolution stated once rather than as a reference-resolution row alone
      |segment[norm-stacking-array] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap :see "[[thoughts-on-multiline-array]]"  Following #result-trace-quotient: one severity row is the sole dissent from what the model already states
      |segment[norm-repair-class] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M3"  Following #der-repair-regimes, with the root-versus-derived bit as the cheaper first form
      |segment[norm-substitution-table] :type normative :max-attainable decided  :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3.11"  Following #der-substitution-table: the two knobs, and where the table lives
      |segment[norm-recognition-profile] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §6.10"  Following #obs-host-profile-fork: float profile and Unicode version as a pinnable profile
      |segment[norm-leading-zero] :type normative :max-attainable decided    :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §3.1"  The two readings of the explicit-base rule the source records, and which remaining case each closes

    |chapter[territory-paths] :title "What each territory inherits"
      |segment[disc-paths-inheritance] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap   What Part V leaves the paths work, and which of its questions are resolver questions rather than syntax questions
      |segment[disc-schema-inheritance] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade :stage gap   What Parts III, IV and VI leave the schema work: the fence, the three seats, and the adoption order
      |segment[disc-dialect-inheritance] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade :stage gap :see "[[dialects-ideation]] §1"  What Part V leaves the dialect work: four jobs separated by when they act, and the fork a named falsifier would settle
      |segment[disc-typing-inheritance] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade :stage gap   What Part III leaves the typing work, and where dialects enter the value lattice
      |segment[disc-embed-inheritance] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade   :stage gap   What Part V leaves embed dispatch, and whether a verbatim label is a registry lookup rather than a second mechanism
      |segment[disc-tooling-inheritance] :type discussion :max-attainable discussion-grade :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §IV"  What Parts IV and VII leave the tooling work: which verbs fall out of the algebra and what each already rests on
      |gap :candidate disc-corpus-boundary-with-udon-needs  Where this corpus ends and the demand evidence begins is undrawn; they touch at typing, addressing and schema


  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |appendix[no-gos] :title "No-gos"
    |arc
      Results that establish something cannot be done. They sit in an appendix rather than a
      chapter because they are evidence other claims consume: a body segment cites a no-go to
      justify a fence it draws, so the dependency runs body-to-appendix while the build order
      runs the other way. Grouping them as a topic made them look like a subject of study; they
      are load-bearing detail for claims stated elsewhere. Each names what it forecloses and
      what would reopen it.

    |segment[app-inference-floor] :type derived :max-attainable exact      :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §6 N1"  Why no proper generalization of a corpus is least among the alternatives, and what the inference floor forces on an extractor
    |segment[app-interleave-cliff] :type derived :max-attainable exact      :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §6 N2"  The complexity cliff for interleaved ordered sub-patterns, and the counting-only fence that keeps the shorthand cheap
    |segment[app-cross-key-order] :type derived :max-attainable exact     :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §6 N3"  What per-key-pair order sensitivity costs, and why the election is whole-row or nothing
    |segment[app-prose-order] :type derived :max-attainable robust-qualitative      :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §6 N4"  Why segment order inside flow is not typeable by the format's own text law, and how far the algebra does reach into prose
    |segment[app-slow-layer-limit] :type derived :max-attainable robust-qualitative    :stage gap :see "[[MINEFIELD-MAP]] §M5"  Which condition slowing the slow layer addresses, and what it buys when the fast layer has no settled state
    |segment[app-second-store] :type derived :max-attainable conditional      :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §1"  Why a materialized index is a correlated channel under persistent bias, and what that forces on corpus verbs
  ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  |appendix[retired] :title "Retired paths"
    |arc
      Refuted material with its refutation, kept so it is not re-walked. A row leaves this
      appendix when its refutation has been worked into a stated no-go, at which point it is
      present-truth content and belongs in the part it constrains.

    |segment[app-atomicity-coherence] :type detail :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[acid-for-corpora]] §2"  The identification of coherence with atomicity, the quantifier on which it was refuted, and what replaced it
    |segment[app-monotonic-debt] :type detail :max-attainable empirical       :stage gap :see "[[schema-ideation]] §1.0"  Absent contraction read as a law, and the corpus's own thesis that contradicts it
    |segment[app-membrane-recurrences] :type detail :max-attainable empirical :stage gap :see "[[db-theory]] §3"  A count of five recurrences narrowed to four when two proved horizontal, and what the narrowing established
    |segment[app-glob-prohibition] :type detail :max-attainable empirical       :stage gap :see "[[DISCUSSION-THOUGHTS]] O14"  A selector prohibition traced to a spelling incumbency, and the three candidate readings that replaced it
    |segment[app-flag-key-foreclosure] :type detail :max-attainable empirical       :stage gap :see "[[type-algebra]] §6 N6"  The flag-key collision read as a foreclosure, the consumer count that refuted it, and the mechanism by which a spike ratified what it was told to ignore
    |segment[app-checker-first] :type detail :max-attainable empirical       :stage gap   Building a conformance checker before the foundation, and why priming forecloses the work it would support
